{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792499821683,"sessionID":"ses_ea6b00f17ffeff7dL3uZ3RUV3t","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b4b0d5475e2e6798","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_bfs_s1/program.md"},"output":"<path>/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_bfs_s1/program.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # evoalgo: Dynamic BFS tree maintenance\n2: \n3: You are searching for a correct, efficient dynamic graph algorithm.\n4: You edit one file, `candidate.py`, and grade it with `evoalgo check`.\n5: \n6: ## Problem\n7: \n8: - Graph: directed; root: yes, vertex 0.\n9: - Implement `init(graph)`, `insert_edge(graph, edge, state)`, `delete_edge(graph, edge, state)`; each returns the new state.\n10: - `graph` is the 0/1 adjacency matrix (list of lists) after the update; `edge` is `(u, v)`. One update flips one entry.\n11: - No globals, no module-level mutable state, standard library only.\n12: \n13: State contract (what the grader reads):\n14: \n15: ```text\n16: Return a dict with 'parent' and 'distances' (lists indexed by vertex, or dicts).\n17: parent[root] = None; parent[v] = None for unreachable v.\n18: distances[v] = BFS distance from the root; -1 or None for unreachable v.\n19: Other keys are allowed and ignored.\n20: ```\n21: \n22: ## Goal for this run\n23: \n24: Reach full correctness first, then avoid a full recompute after updates.\n25: \n26: ## Loop\n27: \n28: 1. Edit `candidate.py`.\n29: 2. Run `evoalgo check` (default level `search`: all non-isomorphic graphs\n30:    up to n = 4, seconds). Use `--level examples` for a faster n <= 3 check.\n31: 3. Read the JSON verdict. On FAIL, `first_failure` names the graph, the\n32:    operation and what was wrong; fix the cause, not the symptom.\n33: 4. Keep a change if it fixes a failure or lowers `work_ratio` without\n34:    breaking correctness; otherwise revert it.\n35: 5. Every check is logged to `results.tsv` and the run folder; do not edit them.\n36: 6. When the search grade passes and you cannot lower `work_ratio` further,\n37:    run `evoalgo check --final` once (n <= 5, minutes) and report.\n38: \n39: Correct first, then cheaper. Prefer the simpler of two equally good versions.\n40: A recompute-from-scratch solution is correct but has `work_ratio` near 1;\n41: the research question is how far below that you can go.\n42: \n43: ## Research brief\n44: \n45: # Research brief: Dynamic BFS tree maintenance\n46: \n47: Research question: can a BFS tree from a fixed root be maintained incrementally\n48: under edge insertions and deletions, with work well below full recomputation?\n49: \n50: The graph is directed; a fixed root r is given. After every update the state\n51: must contain a valid BFS tree: it spans exactly the vertices reachable from r,\n52: every non-root vertex has one parent that is a true in-neighbor, and the\n53: parent of v sits one BFS distance closer to r than v does.\n54: \n55: Known territory: Frigioni et al. give a semi-dynamic BFS algorithm with O(mn)\n56: total work; the fully dynamic case is harder. A structurally different correct\n57: approach is interesting even where a known solution exists.\n58: \n59: Interface reminder: implement init(graph), insert_edge(graph, edge, state),\n60: delete_edge(graph, edge, state). The state is passed in and returned; keep no\n61: globals. Graphs are 0/1 adjacency matrices, vertex ids 0-based. Correctness is\n62: checked by the invariant above, not by equality with a specific tree.\n63: \n64: This is a calibration problem: the system should solve it without help, and it\n65: validates the pipeline end to end.\n\n(End of file - total 65 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# evoalgo: Dynamic BFS tree maintenance\n\nYou are searching for a correct, efficient dynamic graph algorithm.\nYou edit one file, `candidate.py`, and grade it with `evoalgo check`.\n\n## Problem\n\n- Graph: directed; root: yes, vertex 0.\n- Implement `init(graph)`, `insert_edge(graph, edge, state)`, `delete_edge(graph, edge, state)`; each returns the new state.\n- `graph` is the 0/1 adjacency matrix (list of lists) after the update; `edge` is `(u, v)`. One update flips one entry.\n- No globals, no module-level mutable state, standard library only.\n\nState contract (what the grader reads):\n\n```text\nReturn a dict with 'parent' and 'distances' (lists indexed by vertex, or dicts).\nparent[root] = None; parent[v] = None for unreachable v.\ndistances[v] = BFS distance from the root; -1 or None for unreachable v.\nOther keys are allowed and ignored.\n```","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_bfs_s1/program.md","text":"# evoalgo: Dynamic BFS tree maintenance\n\nYou are searching for a correct, efficient dynamic graph algorithm.\nYou edit one file, `candidate.py`, and grade it with `evoalgo check`.\n\n## Problem\n\n- Graph: directed; root: yes, vertex 0.\n- Implement `init(graph)`, `insert_edge(graph, edge, state)`, `delete_edge(graph, edge, state)`; each returns the new state.\n- `graph` is the 0/1 adjacency matrix (list of lists) after the update; `edge` is `(u, v)`. One update flips one entry.\n- No globals, no module-level mutable state, standard library only.\n\nState contract (what the grader reads):\n\n```text\nReturn a dict with 'parent' and 'distances' (lists indexed by vertex, or dicts).\nparent[root] = None; parent[v] = None for unreachable v.\ndistances[v] = BFS distance from the root; -1 or None for unreachable v.\nOther keys are allowed and ignored.\n```\n\n## Goal for this run\n\nReach full correctness first, then avoid a full recompute after updates.\n\n## Loop\n\n1. Edit `candidate.py`.\n2. Run `evoalgo check` (default level `search`: all non-isomorphic graphs\n   up to n = 4, seconds). Use `--level examples` for a faster n <= 3 check.\n3. Read the JSON verdict. On FAIL, `first_failure` names the graph, the\n   operation and what was wrong; fix the cause, not the symptom.\n4. Keep a change if it fixes a failure or lowers `work_ratio` without\n   breaking correctness; otherwise revert it.\n5. Every check is logged to `results.tsv` and the run folder; do not edit them.\n6. When the search grade passes and you cannot lower `work_ratio` further,\n   run `evoalgo check --final` once (n <= 5, minutes) and report.\n\nCorrect first, then cheaper. Prefer the simpler of two equally good versions.\nA recompute-from-scratch solution is correct but has `work_ratio` near 1;\nthe research question is how far below that you can go.\n\n## Research brief\n\n# Research brief: Dynamic BFS tree maintenance\n\nResearch question: can a BFS tree from a fixed root be maintained incrementally\nunder edge insertions and deletions, with work well below full recomputation?\n\nThe graph is directed; a fixed root r is given. After every update the state\nmust contain a valid BFS tree: it spans exactly the vertices reachable from r,\nevery non-root vertex has one parent that is a true in-neighbor, and the\nparent of v sits one BFS distance closer to r than v does.\n\nKnown territory: Frigioni et al. give a semi-dynamic BFS algorithm with O(mn)\ntotal work; the fully dynamic case is harder. A structurally different correct\napproach is interesting even where a known solution exists.\n\nInterface reminder: implement init(graph), insert_edge(graph, edge, state),\ndelete_edge(graph, edge, state). The state is passed in and returned; keep no\nglobals. Graphs are 0/1 adjacency matrices, vertex ids 0-based. Correctness is\nchecked by the invariant above, not by equality with a specific tree.\n\nThis is a calibration problem: the system should solve it without help, and it\nvalidates the pipeline end to end.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":65,"totalLines":65,"truncated":false}},"title":"runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_bfs_s1/program.md","time":{"start":1792499821640,"end":1792499821676}},"id":"prt_15950aa39001H2NoXPtMnAUIgR","sessionID":"ses_ea6b00f17ffeff7dL3uZ3RUV3t","messageID":"msg_159509af0001SMWboJ3sNHvr0P"}}
