{"type":"text","timestamp":1792501367819,"sessionID":"ses_ea6b00f17ffeff7dL3uZ3RUV3t","part":{"id":"prt_15968334f0013RM7lHrnDRdxEQ","messageID":"msg_159682c9e001GWnHKZ4aIp2rFg","sessionID":"ses_ea6b00f17ffeff7dL3uZ3RUV3t","type":"text","text":"\n\nDone. Final verdict: **PASS** on all 4,187,856 tests (n≤5, every non-isomorphic graph, every single edge flip plus update sequences).\n\n## Result\n- **Final grade:** PASS, fitness 1.0, **work_ratio 0.242** (vs 1.0 for a full recompute — updates do ~24% of the offline work).\n- **Workspace:** `/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_bfs_s1`\n- **Checks used:** 5 (2 examples, 2 search, 1 final) — under the 8-check budget.\n\n## The idea (two lines)\nOn **insert**, an edge can only *decrease* distances, so I relax outward from the target via a shortest-path propagation (Dijkstra-lite) — no full recompute. On **delete**, if the edge wasn't the vertex's tree edge nothing changes; otherwise I recompute only the affected BFS-tree subtree with a Dijkstra that pins the unchanged outside distances, and record parents during the relaxation.\n\n## Notes\n- The one real bug I hit: treating `distances[v] == -1` (unreachable) as a numeric value, so a newly-reachable vertex was never updated. Fixed by special-casing `-1` as infinity in the insert comparison.\n- The remaining ratio is dominated by Python-loop overhead against the numpy baseline; at n≤5 the affected-region repair is the dominant cost and it's already well below full recompute, so I stopped optimizing there.","time":{"start":1792501363535,"end":1792501367808}}}
